  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure builds over the Great Lakes this morning, allowing \ndrier air to advect into the region. Any lingering showers will\ndissipate as clouds gradually move east and skies clear. Patchy\nfog may develop towards sunrise as winds become light, but \nshould quickly burn off shorty after sunrise. Northwest flow \nhas also ushered cooler air into the region, with temperatures \nstarting out in the 40s this morning. Despite plenty of \nsunshine, highs will only climb into the 60s today. High \npressure remains in control through Sunday morning, brining dry \nconditions and cooler than normal temperatures. A ridge begins \nto build into the region Sunday, allowing winds to veer to the \nsouth/southwest. Despite the shift in flow, temperatures will \nremain in the 60s across the area.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nModel guidance shows two features moving east Sunday afternoon into \nSunday night, with one passing through the Great Lakes into Canada \nand the other tracking along the eastern seaboard. Our area \nwill remain between these features, which should limit \nprecipitation development to mainly a low end chance of rain \nshowers across eastern portions of the area. The shortwaves are \nforecast to phase well east of the region Sunday night into \nMonday, generating a surface low off the New England coast that\nwill track into Canada. Confidence continues to increase that \nwidespread rain will not develop Sunday.\n\nA cold front will move through Monday, ushering in the coldest air \nmass of the season so far. Temperatures at 850 mb near 0C \ncombined with relatively warm lake temperatures will support \nlake effect clouds and a few isolated rain showers, while \ncontinued CAA will keep temperatures well below average through\nTuesday. Highs Monday will generally reach the upper 50s with \nlower 60s in the valleys of the Southern Tier, NEPA and \nCatskills, followed by highs mainly in the low to mid 50s \nTuesday. Overnight lows will fall into the low to mid 30s Monday\nand Tuesday nights, with some locations potentially reaching \nfreezing, especially Tuesday night. Those with sensitive outdoor\nvegetation should take appropriate precautions. By Wednesday, \nthe ridge axis will shift east and southwest flow will return, \nbeginning a warming trend that should bring temperatures back \ntoward average by the end of the week, with dry conditions \nprevailing.",
